{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Verbs have endings",
      "body": "Spanish verbs come in three groups, by the ending of the infinitive (the \"to …\" form): *-ar* (*hablar*, to speak), *-er* (*comer*, to eat) and *-ir* (*vivir*, to live). To conjugate a regular verb, remove the ending and add the ending for the person.\n\n*-ar* verbs are the biggest group. Here is *hablar*:",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Pronoun", "Ending", "hablar"],
        "rows": [
          ["*yo*", "-o", "*hablo*"],
          ["*tú*", "-as", "*hablas*"],
          ["*él / ella / usted*", "-a", "*habla*"],
          ["*nosotros / nosotras*", "-amos", "*hablamos*"],
          ["*ustedes*", "-an", "*hablan*"],
          ["*ellos / ellas*", "-an", "*hablan*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "One form, three meanings",
      "body": "The Spanish present covers what English says in several ways: *Hablo español* = I speak Spanish / I'm speaking Spanish / I do speak Spanish. It also works for the near future: *Mañana trabajo* (I'm working tomorrow).\n\nTo make it negative, put *no* before the verb: *No hablo francés.* (I don't speak French.) To make a yes/no question, just use question marks and rising intonation: *¿Hablas inglés?*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Common -ar verbs",
      "body": "All the verbs in this lesson's vocabulary follow exactly the *hablar* pattern: *trabajo, trabajas, trabaja, trabajamos, trabajan*. *Estudio, estudias, estudia…* *Escucho, escuchas, escucha…*\n\nFor languages, use the masculine singular with a small letter: *el inglés, el español, el francés*. After *hablar* you normally drop the article: *Hablo inglés.*"
    }
  ],
